WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.080
Welcome to this setup explainer.

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.000
We are smoothly moving right along into

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.000
the next phase of your setup process.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.000
This is step four.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.520
Right now, your AI can find

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.040
any word you've ever typed.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.640
And well, that's really good.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.080
But we all know you don't always

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.000
use the exact same words twice.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.360
So this step adds a second

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.440
way to search, by meaning.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.240
Let's look at one real example of how this works.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:23.840
Say you ask your AI, how

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.920
do I like complicated things explained?

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.480
But the actual conversation you're looking for says,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.720
Show me an example before the instructions.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.920
You see, they're different words, but the exact same idea.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.480
A strict word search just misses it completely.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.120
Meaning search, though, catches it right away.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.960
Once this step is complete, you'll

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.320
have two ways to search.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.280
First, matching words, which alreks for you.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.880
And second, similar ideas, which this new step adds.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.560
Just one honest note here.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.040
A likely match is simply a place to look.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.640
It's not absolute proof that the

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.720
AI's answer is perfectly right.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.640
You'll always get the original passage, so

00:00:58.640 --> 00:00:59.840
you can check it for yourself.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:02.430
Now adding this involves two jobs.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.990
First, your Mac downloads the software it needs.

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:06.270
It just does this once, if

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:07.550
it doesn't have it already.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.310
This includes a small text model

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:10.830
and the libraries that run it.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.350
Everything is stored safely in the

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:14.830
program's own folder and nowhere else.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.630
Then it works through your conversation text once,

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.950
just to prepare that second way of searching.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.110
With a big history, that can take a little while,

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.670
but your Mac stays completely usable the whole time.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:27.070
Your estimates for download, storage, and time

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:28.590
are shown right there for you.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:29.950
And this is important.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.750
The preparation happens entirely on your Mac.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:35.710
Nothing is ever sent to an outside AI service.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:38.190
Just to keep the layers straight, this step makes your

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:42.350
AI's episodic memory, its recall of past events, better.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:43.870
It does not write your AI's

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:45.790
constitution or its core rules.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:47.550
That part is coming up next.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.630
And you really don't have to do this today.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.270
The suggested choices are to add it now, leave

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.070
it for later, or just stay with Word search.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:57.070
And don't worry, choosing later doesn't

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:58.670
undo anything you've already done.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:01.260
Add meaning search now, leave it for

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.260
later, or stay with Word Search.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:07.580
Press a button below.
